.sp 1v
.ps 17
.vs 21
.ft B
Extremal trees for the biharmonic index
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
.sp 1v
.sp 0.65v
Draft prepared with AI assistance; authorship to be determined.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Draft manuscript; not peer reviewed.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
Abstract
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
For a connected graph G on n vertices, the biharmonic index is BH(G) = n times the sum of \(*l_i(G)\u-2\d over the nonzero Laplacian eigenvalues. Lin's Conjecture 5.3 states that among trees on n >= 5 vertices the star K_{1,n-1} minimizes BH and the path P_n maximizes it, with equality only at those trees. We give a complete elementary proof. The key identity is BH(T) = N(T)/n, where N(T) is an explicit sum over pairs of edges obtained from the inverse edge Laplacian. The lower bound follows from a cut identity and laminarity of tree cuts; the upper bound follows from a pendant-path shift whose change is a positive polynomial. We also report a Lean 4.34.1 certificate: the endpoint theorem is kernel-checked, and its axiom closure is exactly propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The paper states the limits of the prior-art search and claims no worldwide novelty.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
1. Introduction
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
Let G be a connected graph with n vertices and Laplacian eigenvalues 0 = \(*l_1 < \(*l_2 <= ... <= \(*l_n. The biharmonic index is
.br
.ce 1
BH(G) = (1/2) sum_{u,v} d_B(u,v)\u2\d = n sum_{i=2}\un\d 1/\(*l_i\u2\d,
.br
.sp 0.65v
where d_B is the biharmonic distance. The source presents the index as inspired by the Wiener index W(G) = sum_{u,v} d(u,v) [3].
.br
.sp 0.65v
Lin [1, Conjecture 5.3] conjectured that for every tree T_n on n >= 5 vertices,
.br
.ce 1
BH(K_{1,n-1}) <= BH(T_n) <= BH(P_n),
.br
.sp 0.65v
with equality on the left if and only if T_n = K_{1,n-1}, and on the right if and only if T_n = P_n. The conjecture is a concretization of Problem 6.3 of Wei, Li and Yang [2], which asks for lower and upper bounds on the biharmonic index B(T) of trees of given order and a characterization of the extremal graphs. This is MathDB record 352303.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Lin proves the following partial results. In the proof of Theorem 5.1, Lin derives the exact value BH(S(a,b)) = n\u2\d - 2n + 4ab + (ab+1)\u2\d/n for a double star S(a,b), and then bounds it over a+b=n-2. Theorem 5.2 proves a strict lower bound for trees of large diameter: if n >= 8 and d(T_n) >= \(*p times the fourth root of 7n/8, minus 1, then BH(T_n) > BH(K_{1,n-1}). Theorem 5.4 shows that among connected graphs of diameter two, the star is the unique maximizer. Theorem 5.5 gives exact values or bounds for firefly graphs. The source proves no upper bound for general trees; the right-hand inequality appears there only as part of Conjecture 5.3.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The extremal trees here are the same two trees as in the classical Wiener-index problem, the star and the path. The proof below is close in method to the classical Wiener-index argument, one moment higher: the first-moment edge sum is replaced by a squared edge-pair sum, and the same pendant-path shift is applied. We do not claim that this analogy is a theorem or that it establishes novelty.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Theorem 1.1.
.ft R
For every n >= 5 and every tree T on n vertices,
.br
.ce 1
BH(K_{1,n-1}) <= BH(T) <= BH(P_n),
.br
.sp 0.65v
with equality on the left exactly when T is isomorphic to K_{1,n-1}, and on the right exactly when T is isomorphic to P_n.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 5v
The rest of the paper proves Theorem 1.1 and describes the formal certificate. Section 2 derives the edge-pair formula; Section 3 proves the lower bound and the star case; Section 4 proves the upper bound and the path case; Section 5 quotes the Lean endpoint; Section 6 states the limits.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
2. The edge-pair formula
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
Let T be a tree with n >= 2 vertices. Orient every edge arbitrarily and let B be the n by (n-1) incidence matrix, with B_{v,e} = 1 if v is the head of e, B_{v,e} = -1 if v is the tail, and 0 otherwise. Put L = B B\uT\d and M = B\uT\d B. The columns of B are linearly independent: if Bc = 0, then at each vertex the signed sum of the c_e over incident edges is zero; deleting leaves one by one forces every c_e = 0. Hence M is invertible and the nonzero eigenvalues of L are exactly the eigenvalues of M, with multiplicity. Therefore
.br
.ce 1
sum_{i=2}\un\d \(*l_i\u-2\d = tr(M\u-2\d) = sum_{e,f} (M\u-1\d)_{ef}\u2\d.     (2.1)
.br
.sp 0.65v
For an edge e let A_e be the component of T-e containing the head of e, and put a_e = |A_e|. Define x_e = 1_{A_e} - (a_e/n) 1, where 1 is the all-ones vector. Then sum_v x_e(v) = 0. For f not equal to e, both ends of f lie on the same side of T-e, so (B\uT\d x_e)_f = 0; for f = e, the head lies in A_e and the tail outside, so (B\uT\d x_e)_e = 1. Thus B\uT\d X = I, where X is the matrix with columns x_e. The columns of X lie in the orthogonal complement of 1, which equals the range of B because L = B B\uT\d has kernel the constants and rank n-1. Hence X = B Y for some Y, and I = B\uT\d B Y = M Y, so Y = M\u-1\d and X = B M\u-1\d. Then
.br
.ce 1
X\uT\d X = M\u-1\d B\uT\d B M\u-1\d = M\u-1\d.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Therefore
.br
.ce 1
(M\u-1\d)_{ef} = <x_e,x_f> = |A_e cap A_f| - a_e a_f/n.     (2.2)
.br
.sp 0.65v
For e = f this equals a_e(n-a_e)/n. For e not equal to f orient both edges so that their heads lie on the sides away from each other (reversing an orientation changes only the sign of the entry, and only its square is used); then A_e and A_f are disjoint, and (M\u-1\d)_{ef} = -|C_e||C_f|/n, where C_e and C_f are the two outer components of T minus {e,f}. Define
.br
.ce 1
N(T) = sum_e [a_e(n-a_e)]\u2\d + 2 sum_{e<f} (|C_e||C_f|)\u2\d.     (2.3)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Combining (2.1), (2.2) and (2.3) with the definition BH(T) = n sum \(*l_i\u-2\d gives
.br
.ce 1
BH(T) = N(T)/n.     (2.4)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Indeed, the diagonal terms contribute n sum_e (a_e(n-a_e)/n)\u2\d = (1/n) sum_e [a_e(n-a_e)]\u2\d, and the ordered off-diagonal pairs contribute n times 2 sum_{e<f} (|C_e||C_f|/n)\u2\d = (2/n) sum_{e<f} (|C_e||C_f|)\u2\d.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Remark 2.1.
.ft R
For edges e,f let w(e,f) be the number of unordered vertex pairs whose T-path uses both e and f. Then w(e,e) = a_e(n-a_e) and w(e,f) = |C_e||C_f| for e not equal to f. Thus N(T) = sum_{e,f} w(e,f)\u2\d, which is also the sum over ordered pairs of vertex pairs of the square of the number of edges common to their paths.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
3. Lower bound and star equality
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
For edges e,f choose sides S_e and S_f and set
.br
.ce 1
tau(S_e,S_f) = (n|S_e cap S_f| - |S_e||S_f|)\u2\d.
.br
.sp 0.65v
By (2.2), (M\u-1\d)_{ef}\u2\d = tau(S_e,S_f)/n\u2\d, so
.br
.ce 1
BH(T) = (1/n) sum_{e,f} tau(S_e,S_f).     (3.1)
.br
.sp 0.65v
The value does not depend on the choices: replacing S_e by its complement changes n|S_e cap S_f| - |S_e||S_f| to its negative, and the square is unchanged.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Cut identity.
.ft R
For subsets A and B of V let
.br
.ce 1
w = |A cap B|,  x = |A cap B\uc\d|,  y = |A\uc\d cap B|,  z = |A\uc\d cap B\uc\d|.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Then n = w+x+y+z, |A| = w+x, |B| = w+y, and
.br
.ce 1
n w - |A||B| = w(w+x+y+z) - (w+x)(w+y) = w z - x y.     (3.2)
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Laminarity.
.ft R
For distinct edges e,f, the forest T minus {e,f} has exactly three components C_e, C_f and M, where C_e meets e only and C_f meets f only. Choosing A = C_e and B = C_f gives A cap B empty, so w = 0 and tau = (x y)\u2\d = |C_e|\u2\d |C_f|\u2\d >= 1. More generally, for any choice of sides, the two cuts are laminar: at least one of the four cells w, x, y, z is empty. For e not equal to f, one of the following holds:
.br
.sp 0.65v
If w = 0, then tau = (x y)\u2\d with x = |A| >= 1 and y = |B| >= 1.
.br
.sp 0.65v
If x = 0, then A is contained in B and tau = (w z)\u2\d with w = |A| >= 1 and z = n-|B| >= 1.
.br
.sp 0.65v
If y = 0, the situation is symmetric.
.br
.sp 0.65v
If z = 0, then A union B = V and tau = (x y)\u2\d with x = |A minus B| >= 1 and y = |B minus A| >= 1, since A and B are proper sides.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Thus tau(S_e,S_f) >= 1 for e not equal to f. If e = f, then x = y = 0 and tau = (w z)\u2\d = [a_e(n-a_e)]\u2\d.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Since 1 <= a_e <= n-1,
.br
.ce 1
a_e(n-a_e) >= n-1,     (3.3)
.br
.sp 0.65v
with equality if and only if a_e is 1 or n-1, that is, if and only if e is pendant. Therefore
.br
.ce 1
BH(T) >= (1/n)[(n-1)(n-1)\u2\d + (n-1)(n-2)] = [(n-1)\u3\d+(n-1)(n-2)]/n = BH(K_{1,n-1}).     (3.4)
.br
.sp 0.65v
The first term counts the n-1 diagonal pairs, each at least (n-1)\u2\d; the second counts the (n-1)(n-2) ordered off-diagonal pairs, each at least 1.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Equality in (3.4) requires equality in every diagonal term and in every off-diagonal term. By (3.3), every edge must be pendant. A tree with every edge pendant is a star for n >= 3: if T were not a star, it would contain a path v_0 v_1 v_2 v_3, and the middle edge v_1 v_2 would have at least two vertices on each side, so it would not be pendant. Conversely, in K_{1,n-1} every edge is pendant, all off-diagonal tau are 1, and BH(K_{1,n-1}) = n\u2\d - 2n + 1/n. Hence equality on the left holds exactly for the star.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
4. Upper bound and path equality
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
Let T be a non-path tree on n vertices. Root T at a leaf rho and let w be a vertex of degree at least 3 at maximum distance from rho. Every child subtree of w is a path; otherwise a vertex of degree at least 3 would lie deeper than w. Since w has a parent and degree at least 3, it has at least two child paths. Choose two of them, with p >= 1 and q >= 1 edges. Let R be the subtree obtained from T by deleting the non-w vertices of these two paths; let r = |V(R)| >= 2 and
.br
.ce 1
S = sum_{e in E(R)} s(e)\u2\d,
.br
.sp 0.65v
where s(e) is the number of vertices on the side of e in R not containing w. Then n = r+p+q.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Let T' be obtained by deleting the q-path at w and attaching it to the far end of the p-path. Then T' is a tree on n vertices with one fewer leaf; w carries a single pendant path of p+q edges.
.br
.sp 0.65v
For a path edge g let j(g) be the number of vertices on its leaf side. Put Q(l) = sum_{j=1}^l j\u2\d and a(j) = j\u2\d + 2Q(j-1) = j(2j\u2\d+1)/3. For a pendant path of length l, the contribution of its internal pairs to N is
.br
.ce 1
F_n(l) = sum_{j=1}^l (n-j)\u2\d a(j).     (4.1)
.br
.sp 0.65v
For the edge with outer size j, the diagonal term is (n-j)\u2\d j\u2\d and the pairs with an edge of outer size i < j contribute 2(n-j)\u2\d i\u2\d; summing over i gives 2Q(j-1).
.br
.sp 0.65v
Compare N(T') and N(T).
.br
.sp 0.65v
(i) For e,f in R, all path vertices lie in the component of w in both trees, so the component sizes of T minus {e,f} are unchanged; the contribution is 0.
.br
.sp 0.65v
(ii) For e in R and a path edge g, the outer components have sizes s(e) and j(g), contributing 2 s(e)\u2\d j(g)\u2\d. In T, j(g) runs over the disjoint union of {1,...,p} and {1,...,q}; in T', over {1,...,p+q}. The total change is 2S(Q(p+q)-Q(p)-Q(q)) = 2S p q (p+q+1).     (4.2)
.br
.sp 0.65v
(iii) Two edges on one pendant path contribute F_n(p)+F_n(q) in T and F_n(p+q) in T'.
.br
.sp 0.65v
(iv) One edge on each of the two paths contributes 2Q(p)Q(q) in T and nothing in T'.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Therefore
.br
.ce 1
N(T')-N(T) = D(n) + 2S p q (p+q+1),     (4.3)
.br
.sp 0.65v
where
.br
.ce 1
D(n) = F_n(p+q)-F_n(p)-F_n(q)-2Q(p)Q(q).     (4.4)
.br
.sp 0.65v
We evaluate D(n). Put n0 = p+q+1. If r = 1, then R = {w}, and both T and T' are the path on n0 vertices; hence N(T) = N(T') and D(n0) = 0. For general r, n = n0+(r-1). Since only F_n depends on n,
.br
.ce 1
D(n) = sum_{k=1}^q [(n-p-k)\u2\d a(p+k)-(n-k)\u2\d a(k)] - 2Q(p)Q(q).     (4.5)
.br
.sp 0.65v
At n0 the same expression is zero:
.br
.ce 1
0 = sum_{k=1}^q [(q+1-k)\u2\d a(p+k)-(p+q+1-k)\u2\d a(k)] - 2Q(p)Q(q).     (4.6)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Subtracting (4.6) from (4.5), and writing c = r+1, gives
.br
.ce 1
D(n) = (r-1) sum_{k=1}^q [(c+2q-2k)a(p+k)-(c+2p+2q-2k)a(k)].     (4.7)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Now use
.br
.ce 1
a(p+k)-a(k) = p(2p\u2\d+6pk+6k\u2\d+1)/3,     2p a(k) = (p/3) 2k(2k\u2\d+1).
.br
.sp 0.65v
Let A = 2p\u2\d+1. The bracket in (4.7) equals
.br
.ce 1
(p/3)[(c+2q-2k)(A+6pk+6k\u2\d)-2k(2k\u2\d+1)].     (4.8)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Set B = sum_{k=1}^q (A+6pk+6k\u2\d) = qA+3pq(q+1)+q(q+1)(2q+1). Using sum k = q(q+1)/2, sum k\u2\d = q(q+1)(2q+1)/6 and sum k\u3\d = q\u2\d(q+1)\u2\d/4, the sum G in (4.7) is
.br
.ce 1
G = (c+2q)B - A q(q+1) - 2p q(q+1)(2q+1) - 4q\u2\d(q+1)\u2\d - q(q+1)
.br
.ce 1
  = cB + A q(q-1) + 2pq(q+1)(q-1) - q(q+1)(2q+1)
.br
.ce 1
  = rB + A q\u2\d + pq(q+1)(2q+1)
.br
.ce 1
  = q[ r(2p\u2\d+2q\u2\d+3pq+3p+3q+2) + 2pq(p+q) + 3pq + p + q ].     (4.9)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Hence
.br
.ce 1
D(n) = (r-1)(pq/3)[ r(2p\u2\d+2q\u2\d+3pq+3p+3q+2) + 2pq(p+q) + 3pq + p + q ].     (4.10)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Substituting (4.10) into (4.3),
.br
.ce 1
N(T')-N(T) = (r-1)(pq/3)[ r(2p\u2\d+2q\u2\d+3pq+3p+3q+2) + 2pq(p+q) + 3pq + p + q ]
.br
.ce 1
  + 2S p q (p+q+1).     (4.11)
.br
.sp 0.65v
Since r >= 2, p,q >= 1 and S >= 1, both terms are strictly positive. No relation between p and q is used, and (4.11) is symmetric in p and q.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Hand checks.
.ft R
For K_{1,3} to P_4: r = 2, p = q = 1, S = 1; (4.11) gives (1/3)39+6 = 19, and N(P_4)-N(K_{1,3}) = 52-33 = 19. For the double star S(2,1) to P_5: r = 2, p = 2, q = 1, S = 1; (4.11) gives (2/3)75+16 = 66, and N(P_5)-N(S(2,1)) = 190-124 = 66. For the spider with legs 2,2,1 to P_6: r = 2, p = q = 2, S = 1; (4.11) gives (4/3)132+40 = 216, and N(P_6)-N(spider) = 553-337 = 216. For K_{1,4} to the fork with legs 2,1,1: r = 3, p = q = 1, S = 2; (4.11) gives 2(1/3)54+12 = 48, and N(fork)-N(K_{1,4}) = 124-76 = 48.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Iterating the shift.
.ft R
Each shift lowers the number of leaves by exactly one and strictly increases N. A tree with two leaves is a path. Starting from any non-path T, after finitely many shifts we reach P_n, and N(T) < N(P_n). By (2.4), BH(T) < BH(P_n). Equality BH(T) = BH(P_n) holds exactly when T is a path. This proves the right-hand inequality and its equality case.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft B
Remark 4.1.
.ft R
The Lean development uses a more general shift: the chain starts at any leaf, and the moved part is an arbitrary pendant subtree Q, not necessarily a path. Only the chain edges change sides, each gaining Q. This generality simplifies the formal bookkeeping; we do not claim that it yields a stronger theorem, since the ordinary proof above already covers the full statement.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
5. Lean certificate
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
The formal certificate is for Lean 4.34.1 with mathlib revision d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612. The audit records the Lean version 4.34.1 and githash 5045d0056413266e57c625dcd7c365b10e377c52.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The definitions are quoted from the module BiHBase. We write Nat and Real for the Lean notations for the natural numbers and the real numbers, which the typesetting font does not provide; all other tokens are reproduced verbatim, with line breaks adjusted to the page.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft CW
.ps 9
.vs 11
noncomputable abbrev lapEigs (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :
    V → Real :=
  (G.isHermitian_lapMatrix Real).eigenvalues
.br
.sp 0.65v
noncomputable def bh (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Real :=
  (Fintype.card V : Real) *
    ∑ i : V, if lapEigs G i = 0 then 0 else (lapEigs G i)\u-1\d ^ 2
.br
.sp 0.65v
noncomputable def bhStar (n : Nat) (hn : 0 < n) : Real :=
  bh (starGraphFin n hn)
.br
.sp 0.65v
noncomputable def bhPath (n : Nat) : Real :=
  bh (pathGraphFin n)
.br
.sp 0.65v
abbrev starGraphFin (n : Nat) (hn : 0 < n) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) :=
  SimpleGraph.starGraph ⟨0, hn⟩
.br
.sp 0.65v
abbrev pathGraphFin (n : Nat) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) :=
  SimpleGraph.pathGraph n
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
The endpoint is quoted from the module BiHEndpoint:
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft CW
.ps 9
.vs 11
theorem bh_endpoint (n : Nat) (hn : 5 ≤ n) (hV : Fintype.card V = n)
    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (hG : G.IsTree) :
    bhStar n (by omega) ≤ bh G ∧ bh G ≤ bhPath n ∧
      (bh G = bhStar n (by omega) ↔ Nonempty (G ≃g starGraphFin n (by omega))) ∧
      (bh G = bhPath n ↔ Nonempty (G ≃g pathGraphFin n)) := by
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
This is the exact statement of Theorem 1.1, with n >= 5, an arbitrary finite vertex type V of cardinality n, and graph isomorphism for the equality cases.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The audit module prints, for each of 26 main theorems including bh_endpoint, the line
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft CW
.ps 9
.vs 11
\&'BiH.bh_endpoint' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
An independent audit by a separate AI reviewer rebuilt all 17 modules from an empty directory, compiled and kernel-checked them in dependency order, scanned the sources for forbidden tokens, walked the transitive constant closure of seven named theorems (36,296 constants, 0 missing), and found exactly the three axioms propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound, with no unsafe or partial declarations. It also checked hand-derived values from the literal eigenvalues, independent of the authors' proof chain: BH(K_2) = 1/2, BH(K_{1,4}) = 76/5, BH(P_5) = 38 and BH(fork) = 124/5, with 76/5 < 124/5 < 38.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Only the spectral form of BH is formalized. The source also prints the double-distance form (1/2) sum d_B(u,v)\u2\d; the equivalence with n sum \(*l_i\u-2\d is the routine identity involving the Moore-Penrose inverse and is not formalized. The upstream mathlib oleans are trusted, as the audit records; the audit lane had no git access, and the mathlib checkout was confirmed separately to be at the stated revision.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
6. Limits
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
The prior-art search was bounded and used no citation index. We did not read Zhou (LAA 429, 2008), Liu-Liu (AML 24, 2011), Das-Xu-Liu (LAA 439, 2013) or Chen-Das (DM 338, 2015). A two-page item at MATCH 91(2) 285-286 surfaced in a search; it could not be read and is unidentified, so we do not know whether it bears on this problem. Gutman's 2004 hyper-Wiener paper was seen in abstract only.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The proof is short and elementary. It is close in method to the classical Wiener-index argument one moment higher, and it introduces no new invariant. The star half is easy. We do not claim worldwide novelty, priority or peer review. This draft was prepared with AI assistance, and the Lean development and its audit were also produced with AI assistance.
